   642	
   643	It was the factor. There was the leather apron, and the slate on its cord, and the pair of pale eyes that had gone along six people and a healer at a gate and counted them. They counted now. They went from a quiet man, who had come up level with the cobs, to four with loads; and they rested last upon the tail-board, where a pair of raw hands lay on an ash stick.
   644	
   645	"Five," said the factor. "And the one that walked behind. He's not behind today."
   646	
   647	"He's paid for where he's sitting," said Brom.
   648	
   649	"I'd not doubt it. He paid my woman a copper to be told where the straw was." The crook went out toward the moor. "Those are mine. I've not had a ewe on this top since the first frost, and I'd three hundred lambed in the yard for want of it. I've had thirty-one carts up that cut in a fortnight, where I told you thirty. The salting-shed's full to the door."
   650	
   651	Nobody answered, since it was a road. But they were at the lip of a hold's own ground, with the hold's keeper speaking, and Cael judged that the rule would bear a low voice there, as it had borne one in that yard.
