   891	## CHAPTER — editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md
   892	
   893	# Chapter 44 — A Wall's Count
   894	
   895	Teague counted them off the scow, from the window on the rise, as he counted anything that came over that river.
   896	
   897	There were six, and there was no mule; and he marked the mule's absence first. A crew that went west with a beast and came home without one had sold it, or eaten it, or lent it; and that crew did not sell what belonged to a healer. So she was off on her own road, with the beast under her, and they had carried three boxes on their backs for as long as she had been gone.
   898	
   899	They carried them poorly: the big one had his on the right shoulder, which was the bound one, and neither of his arms came up above the elbow when he shifted it. The small pale one walked with her left hand laid flat against her coat. The girl had both her arms, which was new since the thaw; she used the left as a person uses a tool that has just come back from the smith. The boy with the book was sound, and came last but one, as he always had.
   900	
   901	Last of all a stick came off the planks, and a stiff leg after it, and then the old man.
   902	
   903	Teague looked at him longer than at the rest.
   904	
   905	He had been home from the shoulder hold for a week. He had taken that salt up himself, behind the first carts, when he might have sent it with the bow and saved his own boots; the bow had said so, and had been right. But a cut that had eaten three carts in one cold term had been opened in an afternoon by the eleventh crew on Pike's cloth, and Teague had wished to see the ground.
   906	
   907	There had been little to see, since it was a wet road between two banks. He had stood in it for a quarter of an hour, and then he had gone up to the gate, where a grey factor with a crook was counting sacks; and he had asked what was done there, and by whom.
   908	
   909	He had been told about a rock, and a knife, and a boy who lit a thing no bigger than a barley grain and walked away from it. He had expected as much, since those five fought as he would have had his own fight, with nothing showing. Then the factor told him the rest.
   910	
   911	Eight had come down that road at a run, and had stopped in the air.
   912	
   913	He had made the factor say it twice. The old man with the stick had stepped out, and struck the ice once, and there had been a thing across the cut from one bank to the other, as high as the scree. Eight hounds broke on it. It stood for exactly as long as the man never lifted a foot; and when it went, he went down with it, like a tent with the pole drawn.
   914	
   915	Teague had never seen an Iron Wall. He had heard of them, as a man who works the holds hears of what is kept behind the walls of towns. They belonged to tribunals, and a person did not come by one in a meadow. Whoever carried such a thing up a border road on a stick had been somebody, a long way from here, and had left off being it; and Teague was not such a fool as to ask what.
   916	
   917	He had asked something else, and it was what a captain needed to know. Could the man move?
   918	
   919	He could not. That was the whole of it, and Teague had carried it home down a moor, turning it over.
   920	
   921	The Force went out along a line and was done. He had been paid for that for the better part of his life, and it had made him first on a sheet of sailcloth: he could put his palm to a thing that six men could not shift, and it shifted. It was a thing for roads: a block came down on a ledge, or a wheel went into a ditch, or a gate would not open; and he arrived, and it was settled, and the wrist kept the account.
   922	
   923	A wall did not arrive. It stayed. It could not come to the trouble; the trouble had to be brought to it, and whoever stood behind it had to be worth the bringing. He had seen that much from a factor's telling; the boy with the book had seen it from two feet away, and had gone and stood there.
   924	
   925	So a wall wanted a count. It was the first thing that such a tool asked of whoever meant to use it: how many are behind? A captain who did not know that figure before the thing went up would learn it afterward, by who was left outside.
   926	
   927	He had it counted before he was off the moor. There were their five, and the old man made six; there were his own three, and himself; and it made ten.
   928	
