+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 42, the “wrong kind of excellent” exchange; chapter 43, “the ground is no farther off, and … it is free”; chapter 46, the mule's “worse company” | The humor belongs to distinct people and often releases pressure without mocking injury. Much of the warmth is indirect, which rewards attention. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 6/10 | Chapter 43, the distant rider from the cairn to the halting-ground; chapter 44, Teague shifting the salt cart once and paying in his wrist | The action is trackable and its costs are clear, but this is predominantly a travel/discovery movement. Suspense rises more through observation than confrontation, and the rider thread pauses after the ferry. | Medium |
+| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Chapter 42, Cael defeating Anchor's pull by counting five piers; chapter 46, Oryn identifying all twelve and the twelfth reading inward | Practice changes what Cael can control and understand. Lira, Brom and Vastin also show visible recovery. Some implications rely on vocabulary learned before this movement. | High |
+| Connection | 7/10 | Chapter 41's daily log, the 15-to-20 recovery count across chapters, and chapter 46's final count of twelve people and a mule | Counts, injuries, tools, debts and repeated phrases carry cleanly across the six chapters. The Tier-B omissions sever several relationship callbacks at exactly their landing points. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 4/10 | Chapter 46, Oryn's forty-minute reading, especially “It does not stop there, and it does not go in”; contrasted with every bracketed Tier-B block | The finished prose has strong cadence and audible character voices. The embedded editorial blocks are unperformable, and some long sentences with multiple pronouns or “thing” referents would be hard to parse by ear. | High |
+
+For this lens, the chief access barrier is not the quietness of the movement. It is the combination of mid-book assumed knowledge, deliberately indirect naming and visible production scaffolding. The concrete work—rope, paper, dividers, injuries, carts—gives a young reader plenty to hold onto when the text remains in scene.
+
+## Adult genre-reader lens
+
