+# Movement 007 cold-read review
+
+**Review status:** Fresh-context simulated cold read, manuscript only. This is an editorial simulation, not a real audience measurement. I read the six supplied files—chapters 41–46—in their declared order and used no project canon.
+
+**Completeness:** The supplied movement is not presentation-complete. Chapters 43–46 contain sixteen literal `[[TIER-B: … coordinator to supply protected text]]` blocks. Several replace decisive dialogue rather than merely annotating it. I have therefore judged the artifact actually supplied, while noting where the surrounding prose shows stronger underlying work.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught in chapter 41 when the skin was turned and the nine-line list appeared. The damaged fourth line gives the research a physical problem, Karis's refusal to convert a plausible word into a finding gives it intellectual stakes, and Cleon's choice to make a second copy gives it human stakes. From there, the strongest hook is not simply *what do the marks mean?* but *what will these exacting people permit themselves to claim?*
+
+Interest slipped first during the long sequence of revisited road landmarks in chapter 42. The bridge, bank, fork-stone and spring all carry emotional accounting, but their similar backward-looking cadence briefly makes the journey feel like a succession of prior-book echoes. The much sharper break comes at the first Tier-B block in chapter 43. From that point onward, production notes repeatedly replace climactic lines—in the cut, at Teague's contract, during Karis's breakthrough, at Oryn's return and after the deep reading. Those interruptions make it impossible to experience several scene landings as written drama.
+
+Vastin matters most in this movement. Cael remains the binding consciousness, but Vastin supplies the most affecting change: he counts his recovery without self-pity, accepts being useful without reclaiming authority, lifts the stick where his wall once stood, learns to sit without payment, and finally adopts another person's word—“Noted”—without turning it into an order. His arc makes the logistical travel feel consequential.
+
+The next event I expect is a field test of the newly combined company before it reaches the oldest site: Teague's file and command, Karis's reading, Vastin's immobile Wall and Cael's twelve carried abilities have been assembled with explicit limits, while the rider behind them remains unresolved. Beyond that immediate pressure, I expect the eastward line to force Cael to decide whether “Still. Set.” is evidence he may share or a warning he must keep.
+
+I would continue. The archive mystery, the practical progression system and the group's restrained care for one another are compelling. I would, however, want the protected-text gaps restored before treating this as a finished reading copy.
+
+## Fluent thirteen-year-old lens
+
+These scores are editorial judgments, not calibrated measurements.
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 7/10 | Chapter 41, opening: “Fifteen” / “Of thirty,” through “There was a day's work between him and a gate” | The count, injury and last-day pressure are concrete immediately. The emotional meaning is intriguing, though a new reader must absorb many unexplained prior circumstances at once. | High |
+| Keep reading | 7/10 | Chapter 46, from the involuntary inward lean to “Still.” and “Set,” then the final eastward departure | The discovery supplies a real payoff and a dangerous secret, and the destination remains open. Repeated manuscript gaps weaken several earlier hooks and payoffs. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 41, Karis and Cleon making two differently useful copies of the damaged fourth line | Research, craft knowledge and epistemic restraint become active scene problems rather than background lore. The material specificity feels unusual and purposeful. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 4/10 | Chapter 43, first Tier-B block at the unmarked place in the cut; chapter 45, the sequence from the three ruled boxes to “Two hundred and six” | The physical props usually make reasoning visible, but sixteen production blocks interrupt syntax and causality. The rapid mark/series/age/bearing deductions in chapter 45 also demand rereading. | High |
+| Character attachment | 8/10 | Chapter 43, Vastin lifting the stick and letting the bound leg bear him; chapter 45, Oryn telling him to stand and his silent compliance | Vulnerability is expressed through choices and bodily behavior. Vastin's pride changes without vanishing, which makes the recovery emotionally credible. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 42, the “wrong kind of excellent” exchange; chapter 43, “the ground is no farther off, and … it is free”; chapter 46, the mule's “worse company” | The humor belongs to distinct people and often releases pressure without mocking injury. Much of the warmth is indirect, which rewards attention. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 6/10 | Chapter 43, the distant rider from the cairn to the halting-ground; chapter 44, Teague shifting the salt cart once and paying in his wrist | The action is trackable and its costs are clear, but this is predominantly a travel/discovery movement. Suspense rises more through observation than confrontation, and the rider thread pauses after the ferry. | Medium |
+| Progression payoff | 8/10 | Chapter 42, Cael defeating Anchor's pull by counting five piers; chapter 46, Oryn identifying all twelve and the twelfth reading inward | Practice changes what Cael can control and understand. Lira, Brom and Vastin also show visible recovery. Some implications rely on vocabulary learned before this movement. | High |
+| Connection | 7/10 | Chapter 41's daily log, the 15-to-20 recovery count across chapters, and chapter 46's final count of twelve people and a mule | Counts, injuries, tools, debts and repeated phrases carry cleanly across the six chapters. The Tier-B omissions sever several relationship callbacks at exactly their landing points. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 4/10 | Chapter 46, Oryn's forty-minute reading, especially “It does not stop there, and it does not go in”; contrasted with every bracketed Tier-B block | The finished prose has strong cadence and audible character voices. The embedded editorial blocks are unperformable, and some long sentences with multiple pronouns or “thing” referents would be hard to parse by ear. | High |
+
+For this lens, the chief access barrier is not the quietness of the movement. It is the combination of mid-book assumed knowledge, deliberately indirect naming and visible production scaffolding. The concrete work—rope, paper, dividers, injuries, carts—gives a young reader plenty to hold onto when the text remains in scene.
+
+## Adult genre-reader lens
+
+| Dimension | Score | Exact passage location | Reason | Confidence |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | 8/10 | Chapter 41, “Fifteen” through the skin being turned to reveal nine scratched lines | The opening joins a personal recovery count, imminent departure and an archaeological reveal without preliminary explanation. It trusts the reader productively. | High |
+| Keep reading | 8/10 | Chapter 45, the divider point falling beyond the hide; chapter 46, “It ran east, off the edge of everything that she had” | The movement pays off the archive work with direction, age and purpose while opening a larger expedition and a private contradiction inside Cael. Missing protected lines prevent the strongest possible score. | High |
+| Interest / freshness | 9/10 | Chapter 41, Cleon's copy “drawn and not read”; chapter 45, Karis refusing to draw a stop the evidence does not give | Bureaucracy, conservation, field craft and power taxonomy all operate as plot. The repeated ethical distinction between observation, inference and ownership is especially fresh. | High |
+| Clarity / flow | 5/10 | Chapter 44, Tier-B contract block through the four spoken terms; chapter 45, the chain from “three marks” to the off-hide point | The underlying cause-and-effect is mostly sound, and concrete objects support abstract deductions. Literal gaps, shifting unnamed speakers and compressed inference create repeated one-pass failures. | High |
+| Character attachment | 9/10 | Chapter 41, Vastin signing the tally and Karis promising to write whose work; chapter 46, Oryn naming the limit of her own Path | Competence, shame, care and autonomy are dramatized rather than announced. Several people make costly choices that are recognizably their own. | High |
+| Humor / warmth | 8/10 | Chapter 42, the gatekeeper reluctantly balancing “six out” against “six in”; chapter 45, Oryn's verdict on Brom's ugly sewing | Dry humor emerges from procedure and character. Warmth is strongest where one person recognizes another's work without sentimentalizing it. | High |
+| Action / suspense | 7/10 | Chapter 43, the return through the cut and the watcher at a measured mile; chapter 44, Teague's Force demonstration and immediate recoil | Position, mechanics and cost are legible. The movement deliberately favors aftermath and preparation over combat, but the watcher and the dangerous eastward bearing sustain pressure. | Medium-high |
+| Progression payoff | 9/10 | Chapter 42, Lira declaring the recovered limb “an arm”; chapter 43, Vastin's unsupported breath and a half; chapter 46, “Still. Set.” | Progress is bodily, technical, relational and epistemic. Abilities reveal limitations at the same time as possibilities, so gains do not erase cost. | High |
+| Connection | 9/10 | Chapter 41's “A lamp, ten days”; chapter 44, Cael reading from Pike's side of the bench; chapter 46, the final company count | Objects, phrases and prior choices recur with altered meaning. The movement converts accumulated debts and observations into a ten-person contract and an eastward route. | High |
+| Read-aloud quality | 6/10 | Chapter 41, “There was a quick small hand…”; chapter 46, the cutwater comparison during Oryn's reading | The prose has controlled cadence, strong consonance and distinct dialogue rhythms. Pronoun-heavy long sentences occasionally blur on first hearing, and the Tier-B blocks destroy oral continuity. | High |
+
+For this lens, the quiet movement feels earned because departure, return and coalition formation all alter status. The prose's reserve is a strength, although it sometimes over-relies on the reader to resolve “he,” “the man,” “the thing” and several nested antecedents while also retaining technical evidence.
